  "keyInsight": "A line integral is the limit of density-times-arc-length Riemann sums as the partition is refined, and that limit is the same no matter which point of each subarc you sample.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Render the left space curve as a smooth blue stroke with a small loop (matching the textbook silhouette) from bottom-left P0 to top-right Pn; place n-1 interior blue dots evenly by arc length, with P1, P2, P3 and P_{n-1} labeled and the rest unlabeled dots. Highlight one kth subarc (e.g. near the loop) and draw the cream zoom arrow to a right-hand inset showing that single subarc enlarged with a red draggable dot P*_k. Use a simple non-constant density such as f(x,y,z) = x (or x+y+z) so that dragging P*_k visibly changes f(P*_k); compute Σ f(P*_k) Δs_k live and display it next to the exact ∫_C f ds so convergence is numerically obvious. Keep the bottom formula box 'ΔM_k ≈ f(x*_k, y*_k, z*_k) Δs_k' always visible. Colors: blue curve/dots, red sample point, cream zoom arrow, gray formula box border."
